@@ -69,6 +69,40 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as text with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The JSON is parsed, re-serialized as multi-line JSON with an indent of 4 spaces by default, and syntax highlighted::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The ``indent`` parameter sets the number of spaces to indent, and ``highlight`` disables syntax highlighting when set to ``False``. Invalid JSON raises the parser's error and prints nothing.
+
+Logging a renderable
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable may also be passed to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('[false, true, null, "foo"]'))
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, but default printing still crops lines wider than the console to its width. To keep long lines whole, enable soft wrapping with ``soft_wrap=True``.
+
+Package level import
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+A package level :func:`rich.print_json` function is also available. It delegates to the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Command line
+~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The ``rich.json`` module may be run as a program to pretty print a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json FILE
+
